  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Field Notebook",
    "overview": "CLF-C02 Drill is a study notebook you carry everywhere. On the phone it fits short, one-handed drills; on the laptop it is the sit-down desk for a full 90-minute simulation and the comparison tables. The page is the Study Desk. Every question, result and table sits on it as a loose sheet. The writing on those sheets carries the design: one question, four or five choices, then the explanation of the trap. Everything else stays out of the way.\n\nThe mood is **encouraging**. The app is coaching someone close to the pass mark, so it should feel like a coach writing in your margin, not a proctor. Encouragement comes from feedback at the right moment: the correct state, the score breakdown, the progress bar filling. It never comes from decorative cheer. Components are **tactile and confident**: generous tap targets, firm fills, and a visible response to every press, because half the sessions happen with a thumb.\n\nThe incumbent build is quieter and flatter than this direction. Panels have only a 1px border, and the moments of encouragement are plain text. Where this document states the direction ahead of the code, it says so.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One accent, Pass-Mark Green, reserved for progress toward passing.",
      "Light and dark palettes that follow the system setting.",
      "Monospace for data and labels, the system font for everything you read.",
      "Sheets on a desk: rounded panels, ruled 1px borders, and soft ambient depth (adopted direction).",
      "Four fixed domain colors that identify the exam domains everywhere."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Pass-Mark Rule",
        "body": "Pass-Mark Green means progress or choice: the primary action, the current selection, a filling bar. It is never decoration, and a screen has one solid green button at most.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Never-Color-Alone Rule",
        "body": "Correct and wrong are always spelled out in text (\"Correct\", \"Not quite. Answer: B\", \"Your answer:\", \"Correct:\"). The red/green wash is reinforcement, never the only signal.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Domain Identity Rule",
        "body": "Each domain color always identifies the same exam domain. Never reuse a domain color for an unrelated meaning on the same screen.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Mono Means Data Rule",
        "body": "Monospace is only for numbers, timers, counts and uppercase labels. A sentence is never set in mono.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Question First Rule",
        "body": "The question stem is the largest text on the quiz screen after the progress line. Nothing in the quiz chrome competes with it.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Column Rule",
        "body": "Every screen is one column of sheets on the desk. No sidebars and no multi-column dashboards, so the phone and laptop layouts are the same layout.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Paper On A Desk Rule",
        "body": "Only sheets (panels, answer options, Compare cards) carry depth. Chips, tags, bars and buttons inside a sheet stay flat.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
